cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If these energies are used in published work, please cite this package and the sources
  listed under `references` — in particular SKEMPI 2.0, which defines every mutation here,
  and FoldX, which computed every value.
title: "skempi-foldx: FoldX binding ddG for SKEMPI 2.0"
abstract: >-
  Precomputed FoldX interaction-energy changes for protein-protein binding mutations defined by
  SKEMPI 2.0, with the campaign pipeline that produced them. Twelve decomposed AnalyseComplex
  terms per mutation -- the total and eleven of its components -- as mutant minus wild-type,
  over 6107 records. Energy values are tied to a
  release: a mutation's ddG depends on the whole mutation list it was computed in.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Chin
    given-names: Connor
    email: cchin29@stanford.edu
    affiliation: Stanford University
version: "0.2.0"
date-released: "2026-09-08"
url: "https://github.com/cchin29/skempi-foldx"
repository-code: "https://github.com/cchin29/skempi-foldx"
# NO `license` OR `license-url` FIELD, deliberately, and this is the one place where saying
# nothing is more accurate than saying something. The repository holds two things: MIT covers the
# code, and the computed results under skempi_foldx/data/ derive from SKEMPI 2.0 and carry its
# CC BY 4.0. pyproject.toml states that correctly as `MIT AND CC-BY-4.0`, and NOTICE draws the
# line file by file.
#
# CFF 1.2 cannot express it. Its schema note on the array form of `license` reads "when there are
# multiple licenses, it is assumed their relationship is OR, not AND" -- so `[MIT, CC-BY-4.0]`
# would publish the claim that a consumer may take the whole work under MIT alone, dropping the
# CC BY attribution that NOTICE says is a licence term rather than a courtesy. Naming one licence
# is equally wrong, in one direction or the other. Omitting the optional field asserts nothing
# untrue and leaves LICENSE, NOTICE and pyproject.toml as the record. (Array values also break
# Zenodo's metadata parser, which matters for the DOI note below.)
#
# No `identifiers:` block yet -- there is no DOI for the software itself. If one is minted from
# a tag, add it here in the same pass.
#
# `version` and `date-released` track the git tag and pyproject.toml together; bump all three in
# the release commit. `issue`, not `number`, for a periodical issue: CFF 1.2 defines `number` as
# the accession number of a work.
keywords:
  - FoldX
  - SKEMPI
  - protein-protein interaction
  - binding affinity
  - ddG
references:
  # Defines every mutation, chain grouping and cleaned mutation string here, and supplies the
  # structures FoldX was run on. CC BY 4.0, so attribution is a licence term.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      SKEMPI 2.0: an updated benchmark of changes in protein–protein binding energy,
      kinetics and thermodynamics upon mutation
    authors:
      - family-names: Jankauskaitė
        given-names: Justina
      - family-names: Jiménez-García
        given-names: Brian
      - family-names: Dapkūnas
        given-names: Justas
      - family-names: Fernández-Recio
        given-names: Juan
      - family-names: Moal
        given-names: Iain H.
    year: 2019
    doi: 10.1093/bioinformatics/bty635
    journal: Bioinformatics
    volume: 35
    issue: 3
    start: 462
    end: 469

  # Produced every number in skempi_foldx/data/.
  - type: article
    title: "FoldX 5.0: working with RNA, small molecules and a new graphical interface"
    authors:
      - family-names: Delgado
        given-names: Javier
      - family-names: Radusky
        given-names: Leandro G.
      - family-names: Cianferoni
        given-names: Damiano
      - family-names: Serrano
        given-names: Luis
    year: 2019
    doi: 10.1093/bioinformatics/btz184
    journal: Bioinformatics
    volume: 35
    issue: 20
    start: 4168
    end: 4169
    notes: >-
      The citable reference for the FoldX suite. The campaign itself ran FoldX 5.1, as NOTICE
      records; the version difference is deliberate rather than a mismatch.

  # The structures the energies were computed on, reached via SKEMPI's cleaned PDB set.
  - type: article
    title: "The Protein Data Bank"
    authors:
      - family-names: Berman
        given-names: Helen M.
      - family-names: Westbrook
        given-names: John
      - family-names: Feng
        given-names: Zukang
      - family-names: Bourne
        given-names: Philip E.
    year: 2000
    doi: 10.1093/nar/28.1.235
    journal: Nucleic Acids Research
    volume: 28
    issue: 1
    start: 235
    end: 242
    notes: >-
      Structures are not redistributed here; the computed outputs are. First three authors plus
      the senior author; see the paper for the full list.

  # Not a dependency, and not the origin of the exclusion either -- cited because it is the
  # published record of a convention this campaign follows. 1KBH is dropped as a whole complex
  # before the worklist is built: RepairPDB does not terminate on it (independently observed at
  # >22.5 h and >10 h on two machines), and RDE-Network's block_list corroborates the choice.
  # exclusions.py records that every block list in every surveyed tree is {'1KBH'} and nothing
  # more, so this is a field-wide convention rather than one group's. The drop removes 86 records
  # outright; it changes no other complex's mutation list, and so moves no shipped value.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Rotamer Density Estimator is an Unsupervised Learner of the Effect of Mutations on
      Protein-Protein Interaction
    authors:
      - family-names: Luo
        given-names: Shitong
      - family-names: Su
        given-names: Yufeng
      - family-names: Wu
        given-names: Zuofan
      - family-names: Su
        given-names: Chenpeng
      - family-names: Peng
        given-names: Jian
      - family-names: Ma
        given-names: Jianzhu
    year: 2023
    doi: 10.1101/2023.02.28.530137
    journal: bioRxiv
    notes: >-
      Also ICLR 2023. See skempi_foldx/exclusions.py for the timing evidence and for why the
      exclusion is independently motivated rather than inherited.

# MuLAN is not listed. This package was extracted from a research fork of it, but none of that
# fork's code is present -- which is what leaves this repository free to carry a permissive
# licence. NOTICE records the relationship under "Related work", the right place for provenance
# that is not a dependency.
